    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki.jpg" alt="Nihon Shoki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nihonshoki_tanaka_version.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shinto has no founder and no moment of beginning. Its name, written with the Chinese characters for “gods” and “way”, Shinto or kami no michi, “the way of the kami”, appears first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> of 720, where it distinguishes the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>’s native gods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, the new religion that had arrived from the continent.</p><p>Its roots are traced to prehistoric Japan, above all to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-farming communities of the Yayoi period, in the last centuries BCE, whose bronze bells and mirrors were used in rituals and whose agricultural cycle shaped the festivals of planting and harvest at the heart of Shinto. In the Kofun period, from the 3rd century CE, powerful clans built great burial mounds, and on the island of Okinoshima, between Japan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, offerings of mirrors, swords, jewels and later gold and ceramics were made to the kami from the 4th to the 9th centuries. A Chinese chronicle of the 3rd century describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himiko">Himiko</a>, a queen of Wa, as a ruler who served the spirits and bewitched the people.</p><p>Each clan, uji, honoured its own kami, often as its ancestor, and the most powerful of them, the clan that became the imperial house, worshipped the sun goddess Amaterasu. Buddhism arrived from the Korean kingdom of Baekje in the 6th century, by tradition in 538 or 552, and after a struggle among the court clans it was adopted by the rulers; from then on the worship of the kami defined itself alongside it.</p><p>In the late 7th and early 8th centuries, the emperor Tenmu and the empress Jitō, building a centralised state on the Chinese model, organised the cult of the kami around the throne. The Grand Shrine of Ise, dedicated to Amaterasu, was raised above all others and rebuilt for the first time in 690; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taihō Code</a> of 701 created a Council of Kami Affairs, the Jingikan, beside the Council of State; and the court commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a> in 712 and the Nihon Shoki in 720, which set down the myths of the kami and the descent of the emperors from the sun goddess.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the heart of Shinto are the kami: gods, spirits and sacred powers, the “eight million kami” of tradition. The 18th-century scholar Motoori Norinaga described as kami anything extraordinary that inspires awe: the sun, the wind and thunder; mountains such as Fuji and Miwa, waterfalls, rocks and ancient trees; the ancestors of clans and families; and outstanding human beings, such as the scholar Sugawara no Michizane, worshipped as Tenjin, the kami of learning, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, enshrined at Nikko.</p><p>The myths of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> tell how the divine pair Izanagi and Izanami created the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; how Izanami died giving birth to fire and Izanagi, returning from the land of the dead, purified himself in a river and gave birth to the sun goddess Amaterasu, the moon god Tsukuyomi and the storm god Susanoo; how Amaterasu hid in a cave and plunged the world into darkness until the other kami lured her out; and how her grandson Ninigi descended to rule the land, bearing the mirror, the sword and the jewel that remain the regalia of the emperors, whose line, according to the myth, descends from him through the first emperor, Jimmu.</p><p>Shinto places great weight on purity. Contact with death, blood and disease brings kegare, pollution, which must be removed by harae, rites of purification, and by misogi, washing in water. Before approaching a shrine, worshippers rinse their hands and mouth at a basin; priests wave a wand of white paper streamers over people and things to purify them.</p><p>The relationship with the kami is one of gratitude, offering and petition. People ask them for protection, good harvests, health, success and safe childbirth, and thank them in festivals, matsuri, in which the kami is carried through the streets in a portable shrine, with music, dance and celebration. Shinto values sincerity of heart, harmony within the community and with nature, and continuity with the ancestors. It has little doctrine about life after death, a realm long left to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>: in Japan, it is often said, people are born Shinto and die Buddhist.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shinto has no revealed scripture or creed. Its foundational texts are chronicles commissioned by the imperial court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a>, “Record of Ancient Matters”, completed in 712, was compiled by the courtier Ō no Yasumaro from the recitations of Hieda no Are; it begins with the first kami, who appeared when heaven and earth separated, and continues the history of the emperors to the early 7th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a>, “Chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>”, completed in 720 and written in classical Chinese, tells the same history at greater length, often recording several versions of each myth.</p><p>Other early sources add local traditions: the Fudoki, provincial gazetteers ordered in 713, record the legends of the regions, and the poems of the Man’yoshu, compiled in the 8th century, celebrate the kami of mountains and the land.</p><p>The Engishiki, a code of procedures completed in 927, preserves the ritual life of the court: its register lists 2,861 shrines receiving offerings from the state, and it records the norito, the ritual prayers addressed to the kami, among them the Great Purification prayer recited twice a year. Norito are still the core of Shinto ritual.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, priests of Ise and later Yoshida Kanetomo, who lived from 1435 to 1511, wrote treatises that gave Shinto a theology of its own. In the 18th century, scholars of the National Learning movement turned to the ancient texts to recover a Japanese spirit free of Chinese and Buddhist influence: Motoori Norinaga spent more than thirty years on his great commentary on the Kojiki, completed in 1798, which made that once-neglected book the central classic of Shinto.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hirohito"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hirohito.jpg" alt="Hirohito" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hirohito_in_dress_uniform_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history Shinto was intertwined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. From the 8th century, Buddhist temples were built at shrines and shrines at temples, and by the Heian period the theory of honji suijaku taught that the kami were local manifestations of the buddhas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">bodhisattvas</a>. Monks served at shrines, and the great kami Hachiman was honoured as a bodhisattva. This combination of kami and buddhas shaped Japanese religion for a thousand years.</p><p>Shinto thinkers gradually claimed an independent identity. Priests of the Outer Shrine of Ise developed a Shinto theology in the 13th and 14th centuries, and Yoshida Kanetomo in the 15th century reversed the old theory, teaching that the buddhas were manifestations of the kami. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, Confucian scholars and the National Learning movement of Motoori Norinaga and Hirata Atsutane exalted the kami and the emperor as the essence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868 put these ideas into practice. The new government ordered the separation of kami and buddhas, provoking a wave of destruction of Buddhist temples, statues and texts, and it made shrine worship a national institution, called State Shinto by historians: shrines were ranked and funded by the state, their priests became officials, the emperor was revered as a living kami, and schoolchildren were taught loyalty to him unto death. Shrine worship was declared a civic duty rather than a religion, while thirteen religious groups, such as Tenrikyo and Konkokyo, were recognised as Sect Shinto. The Yasukuni Shrine, founded in Tokyo in 1869, enshrined those who died fighting for the emperor.</p><p>As Japan built an empire, shrines were raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the Pacific islands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> were forced to worship at them. Japanese emigrants built shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In December 1945 the Allied occupation’s Shinto Directive abolished State Shinto, and on 1 January 1946 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a> issued a rescript generally read as a renunciation of his divinity. The shrines became private religious bodies, most of them joining the Association of Shinto Shrines, founded in 1946, and the 1947 constitution separated religion from the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine.jpg" alt="Ise Grand Shrine" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise Grand Shrine</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naiku_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">N yotarou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> has some 80,000 Shinto shrines, from the great shrines of Ise, Izumo and Meiji to tiny wayside sanctuaries. A shrine is marked by a torii, a gateway; its main hall holds the shintai, the object, often a mirror, in which the kami resides, unseen by worshippers, who pray at a hall of worship in front by bowing, clapping and offering coins. Sacred places, trees and rocks are marked by twisted ropes with paper streamers. Some shrines have no main hall at all, because the kami is the mountain itself, as at Mount Miwa near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>.</p><p>Shrines are tended by priests, kannushi, once often from hereditary families, assisted by shrine maidens, miko, who perform sacred dances, kagura. Each locality has its tutelary kami, whose festival is the great event of the neighbourhood year; networks of shrines honour popular kami such as Inari, the kami of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and prosperity with its fox messengers, and Hachiman, a kami of warriors, each with tens of thousands of branch shrines. Homes may keep a kamidana, a shelf altar for the kami.</p><p>Shinto marks the life of the individual: newborn babies are presented at the local shrine, children of three, five and seven years old are taken there in November, and the Shinto wedding, popularised after the wedding of the crown prince in 1900, remains common. Millions of people visit shrines in the first days of the New Year, buying amulets, drawing fortunes and writing wishes on wooden plaques; new buildings are blessed by priests at groundbreaking ceremonies.</p><p>The emperor remains Shinto’s highest priest in private ritual: he plants and harvests rice in the palace grounds and offers the first fruits to the kami, and each new emperor performs the Great Thanksgiving Festival, last held in 2019. Most Japanese practise Shinto without regarding it as a religion or an exclusive identity, and the same families turn to Buddhist temples for funerals and the memory of the dead.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/itsukushima"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/itsukushima.jpg" alt="Itsukushima" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/itsukushima">Itsukushima</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miyajima_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shinto has shaped Japanese culture so deeply that it is hard to separate from it. Its architecture, from the austere unpainted cypress of Ise and the towering hall of Izumo to the vermilion torii of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itsukushima">Itsukushima</a> rising from the sea, is among the most distinctive in the world. The Noh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> grew from performances at shrine and temple festivals, and sumo began as a ritual entertainment for the kami: its ring is still purified with salt, under a roof built in the style of the shrine of Ise.</p><p>The sacred groves that surround shrines have preserved fragments of ancient forest across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and Shinto’s sense of the sacred in nature has been cited by modern environmentalists. Its imagery fills Japanese literature, art and popular culture, from classical poetry to animated films.</p><p>Shinto’s role in modern nationalism remains its most contested legacy. State Shinto taught generations of Japanese to revere the emperor as divine and served the militarism that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The Yasukuni Shrine, where the souls of Japan’s war dead, including convicted war criminals, are enshrined, remains a source of political controversy when Japanese leaders visit it, provoking protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><p>Since 1945 Shinto has redefined itself as a religion of community, nature and tradition. Its persistence through industrialisation and urbanisation, as a living practice of shrines and festivals in one of the most modern societies on earth, shows how deeply it is rooted in Japanese life.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Shinto, “the way of the kami”, is the native religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>: the worship of the sacred powers of nature, ancestors and great people at shrines, without a founder or a single scripture. Organised around the imperial court in the 7th and 8th centuries and intertwined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> for a thousand years, it was made the creed of the state from 1868 to 1945, and it remains woven into the festivals and life of modern Japan.</em></p>

